WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.237
Before writing an aggregation query, use this five-step workflow.

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:07.447
First, ask the decision question.

00:00:07.447 --> 00:00:10.327
What are we trying to learn from the data?

00:00:10.327 --> 00:00:16.392
Second, set the grain. Decide what one output row should represent.

00:00:16.392 --> 00:00:19.492
One diagnosis? One category? One department?

00:00:19.492 --> 00:00:26.012
Third, choose the measures. That might be a count, a total, an average, a minimum, or a maximum.

00:00:26.012 --> 00:00:30.612
Fourth, decide which rows qualify and which groups qualify.

00:00:30.612 --> 00:00:35.012
This is where WHERE and HAVING play different roles.

00:00:35.012 --> 00:00:38.522
Finally, validate the summary against the detail data.

00:00:38.522 --> 00:00:39.737
The most important step is grain.

00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:46.832
If you cannot describe one result row in a short sentence, do not rush into GROUP BY yet.

00:00:46.832 --> 00:00:49.407
Clarify the intended summary first.

00:00:49.407 --> 00:00:56.607
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:56.607 --> 00:01:01.917
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:06.112
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:06.112 --> 00:01:11.222
In a real setting such as a retail report comparing revenue across products and regions,

00:01:11.222 --> 00:01:19.112
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:21.957
before discussing syntax.

00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:28.475
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
